WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.639
Your program wants to talk to example.com.

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:09.068
But a packet needs a numeric address, not a name.

3
00:00:09.068 --> 00:00:12.917
So the program asks: what's the address of example.com?

4
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:14.820
A moment later, it has two.

5
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.443
One question, one answer.

6
00:00:16.443 --> 00:00:22.676
But behind it, three different organisations were asked, and your program spoke to none of them.

7
00:00:22.676 --> 00:00:23.909
Who did the asking?

8
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.990
In the last section, the names were phone books.

9
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.949
The first book only says which book covers each ending, like com.

10
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.301
The com book only says which book each company keeps.

11
00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:36.959
Only the company's own book has the numbers.

12
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.338
You could read all three yourself.

13
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.524
Most people don't.

14
00:00:40.524 --> 00:00:45.461
They ring directory enquiries: one call, and somebody else reads the books.

15
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.440
Your program is the caller.

16
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.263
Inside it is a small piece of code called the stub resolver; Java's getByName calls it.

17
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.172
It makes one call, to the address in a small settings file.

18
00:00:57.172 --> 00:01:01.392
That address belongs to a recursive resolver: directory enquiries.

19
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:08.784
And the books are servers: the root, the com servers, and example.com's own servers, the authoritative ones.

20
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.115
Why the middleman?

21
00:01:10.115 --> 00:01:13.498
To be fair, your program could walk the books itself.

22
00:01:13.498 --> 00:01:16.816
A tool called dig can do exactly that, and it works.

23
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:24.120
It takes three trips: 46, 48 and 16 milliseconds, about a tenth of a second, for every lookup.

24
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.089
Now multiply.

25
00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:33.752
If every program on the internet started at the root for every lookup, a few root servers would answer for the whole world, all the time.

26
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.478
So the resolver does the walking, and it remembers.

27
00:01:37.478 --> 00:01:38.180
Watch how.

28
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:44.695
The stub sends one question to the resolver, with a flag that means: do the whole job for me.

29
00:01:44.695 --> 00:01:47.008
It's called RD, recursion desired.

30
00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:49.117
The resolver's notes are empty.

31
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.702
So it starts at the top.

32
00:01:50.702 --> 00:01:56.384
A root server's address is built into its software, because nothing can be looked up before it.

33
00:01:56.567 --> 00:02:01.394
It asks the same question, without that flag: just tell me what you know.

34
00:02:01.394 --> 00:02:04.105
Does the root know example.com's address?

35
00:02:04.105 --> 00:02:05.163
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:08.578
No.

37
00:02:08.578 --> 00:02:11.321
The root is like a very senior manager.

38
00:02:11.321 --> 00:02:14.979
It doesn't know the answer, but it knows who to ask.

39
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:20.509
It replies: ask the com servers, here are 13 of them, and here are their addresses.

40
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.144
That reply is a referral.

41
00:02:22.144 --> 00:02:24.981
The addresses that come with it are called glue.

42
00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:31.247
Without them, the resolver would have to look up the com servers' names before it could ask them anything.

43
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.649
The resolver asks a com server.

44
00:02:33.649 --> 00:02:42.720
Another referral, one level down: example.com lives on two servers run by Cloudflare, hera and elliott, with their addresses.

45
00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:44.523
Third question, to hera.

46
00:02:44.523 --> 00:02:49.493
This time, an answer: two addresses, marked authoritative, the original.

47
00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:53.373
Each may be kept for 300 seconds, its time to live.

48
00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:56.999
The resolver passes them to the stub, marked as a copy.

49
00:02:56.999 --> 00:03:00.843
Three trips for the resolver; one question from your program.

50
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.900
Now the names.

51
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.500
The resolver that walks for you is a recursive resolver.

52
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.129
The just-tell-me-what-you-know questions it sends are iterative queries.

53
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.686
And it remembered everything.

54
00:03:12.686 --> 00:03:18.359
The referrals came with a time to live of 172,800 seconds: two days.

55
00:03:18.359 --> 00:03:21.529
The answer, 300 seconds: five minutes.

56
00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:25.734
200 seconds later, another program asks for example.com.

57
00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:32.560
The resolver answers from its notes in one short trip, and says the copy has 100 seconds left.

58
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.554
That's why a few root servers can serve the whole internet.

59
00:03:36.554 --> 00:03:43.479
A busy resolver answers almost every question from its notes, and asks the root again only every two days.

60
00:03:43.479 --> 00:03:44.197
Lucky root.

61
00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:46.587
The catch: every note is a copy.

62
00:03:46.587 --> 00:03:51.512
For up to five minutes, the resolver hands out its copy without asking anyone.

63
00:03:51.512 --> 00:03:55.617
If the owner changes the address, the copies don't hear about it.

64
00:03:55.617 --> 00:03:58.332
Fast answers and old answers come together.

65
00:03:58.467 --> 00:03:59.995
So, who did the asking?

66
00:03:59.995 --> 00:04:01.989
Your program asked one helper.

67
00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:09.964
The helper walked the tree, one referral at a time, and wrote down everything it learned, each note with its own expiry.

68
00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:11.268
One quick check.

69
00:04:11.268 --> 00:04:15.099
Ten minutes later, someone asks for example.com again.

70
00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:18.220
Which servers does the resolver have to ask?

71
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.355
Pause and think.

72
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.956
Only example.com's own servers, hera or elliott.

73
00:04:25.956 --> 00:04:30.710
The five-minute answer has run out, but the referrals last two days.

74
00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.668
The root and com get a rest.
